public String toRegexForFixedDate (Date date){
    StringBuilder buf = new StringBuilder ();
    Converter < Object > p = headTokenConverter;
    while (p != null){
        if (p instanceof LiteralConverter){
            buf.append (p.convert (null));
        } else if (p instanceof IntegerTokenConverter){
            buf.append (FileFinder.regexEscapePath ("(\\d+)"));
        } else if (p instanceof DateTokenConverter){
            DateTokenConverter < Object > dtc = (DateTokenConverter < Object >)p;
            if (dtc.isPrimary ()){
                buf.append (p.convert (date));
            } else {
                buf.append (FileFinder.regexEscapePath (dtc.toRegex ()));
            }
        }
        p = p.getNext ();
    }
    return buf.toString ();
}

So it iterates over a chain of converters (headTokenConverter). For each converter, if it's a LiteralConverter, it appends the literal string. If it's an IntegerTokenConverter, it appends a regex for digits "(\\d+)". If it's a DateTokenConverter, if it's primary, it appends the date formatted by that converter; else it appends the regex representation of that date token converter. It escapes regex path for non-primary date tokens. Finally returns the built string.

Thus the method builds a regex string for a fixed date pattern, using converters to produce parts of the regex. It uses the provided date for primary date tokens.

So summary: "Build a regex string for a fixed date pattern using token converters, inserting the given date for primary date tokens and regex placeholders for other tokens."

